Prove a Property of Logarithms

How can you prove the Product Property of Logarithms?

Let x = \log_b{m} and y = \log_b{n}. Then b^x = m and b^y = n.
\begin{align*}
b^x · b^y &= m · n Multiply the expressions b^x and b^y. 
b^{x+y} &= mn 
x + y &= \log_b{mn} Rewrite the equation in logarithmic form. 
\log_b{m} + \log_b{n} &= \log_b{mn}
\end{align*}

Expand Logarithmic Expressions

How can you use the properties of logarithms to expand each expression?

A. \log_5(a^2b^7)
\begin{align*}
\log_5(a^2b^7) &= \log_5(a^2) + \log_5(b^7) 
&= 2\log_5 a + 7\log_5 b
\end{align*}

B. \ln((25)/(3))
\begin{align*}
\ln((25)/(3)) &= \ln((5^2)/(3)) 
&= \ln(5^2) - \ln 3 
&= 2\ln 5 - \ln 3
\end{align*}

